      "writtenOn": "2023-10-19T01:45:28Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Why does the `get version` request need to go in an encrypted request?\n\nIn the same line of thinking, what is the reason behind the API not exposing three methods for `getVersion`, `store` and `read` and only encrypt the sensitive content of the input/output of `store` and `read`?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 45,
        "startChar": 31,
        "endLine": 45,
        "endChar": 70
      },
      "revId": "5f7b9d0d5deea35f683168ed2f250d3a402bb469",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "4212974b_7cfe3ca5",
        "filename": "security/secretkeeper/aidl/android/hardware/security/secretkeeper/SecretManagement.cddl",
        "patchSetId": 11
      },
      "lineNbr": 45,
      "author": {
        "id": 1897307
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-10-19T21:04:26Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "We decided to do a fully encrypted channel instead of only encrypting sensitive content because we wanted to ensure integrity of entire request along with encryption.\n\nFor ex, an adversary should not be able to pick encrypted secret from a request intended as `store_secret(id, secret, policy)` \u0026 instead trigger `get_secret(id, policy)`. [An alternative would be including opcode an aad but then opcode will need to be bytes \u0026 their wouldn\u0027t be any point doing store()/read() as different AIDL methods]. Also, protocol would be too complex in that case.\n\n\u003e Why does the get version request?\nIf your question extends to why the get version response is encrypted, it is to prevent downgrade attacks of inner APIs such as SecretManagement \u0026 Dice Policy.",
      "parentUuid": "9fa061d4_204f7550",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 45,
        "startChar": 31,
        "endLine": 45,
        "endChar": 70
      },
      "revId": "5f7b9d0d5deea35f683168ed2f250d3a402bb469",
